    ["web_files",
    ".WEBARCHIVEXML",
    "Android Web Browser Archive",
    "2.7",
    "Webpage archive created by the default Web browser on Android mobile devices; saves an offline version of a webpage that may be viewed without being connected to the Internet; saved to the Downloads directory on the Android device and can only be viewed in the Android browser. Android Google Android Browser —  (Discontinued)  Included with OS.",
    "Android Google Android Browser —  (Discontinued)  Included with OS.",
    "?"],
    ["web_files",
    ".ASMX",
    "ASP.NET Web Service File",
    "2.7",
    "Defines web services called by an Active Server Page (.ASP file); automatically compiled by ASP.NET when a request to the service is made; can be accessed over the Internet or from a local network. ASMX files begin with the ASP.NET directive WebService, followed by the Language parameter. This is used to set the programming language to Visual Basic, C#, or JScript. They can be run using a Windows-based web server with the.NET framework. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022.",
    "Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022.",
    "?"],
    ["web_files",
    ".WIDGET",
    "HybridJava Widget",
    "2.7",
    "HTML-based widget used by Windows Mobile, an operating system for PDAs, digital phones, and other mobile devices; contains an archive of files in a compressed Zip format; may store.HTML,.XML,.CSS, and.JS files, as well as other Web-based files. WIDGET files are used for deploying small programs for capabilities such as weather and news reports, maps, and games. NOTE: WIDGET files are actually.ZIP files that have been renamed with the .widget extension. Therefore, you can unpack them by renaming the file extension to .zip and decompressing them using any Zip-compatible decompression utility. Windows Microsoft Windows Mobile SDK. Microsoft Visual Studio with Windows Mobile Developer Tool Kit plug-in. A WIDGET file is a small program (known as a widget), such as a clock, weather forecaster, or countdown timer, run by the Yahoo! Widget Engine. Typically, users double-click the WIDGET file to run it with Yahoo! Widgets on their desktops. However, they can also extract its contents with a Zip utility since WIDGET files are Zip-compressed archives that contain a.KON file, which defines the widget, and resource files, such as images and.JS files, which comprise the widget. WIDGET file open in Yahoo! Widgets 4 on a Windows desktop Yahoo! Widgets was previously Konfabulator until Yahoo! purchased it in 2005, then Yahoo! discontinued the software in 2012. Since Yahoo! Widgets is no longer developed, WIDGET files are obscure. WIDGET File Structure WIDGET files are zipped folders that contain a Contents folder, which typically stores the following: KON file - A file that contains plain text stored in the XML standard that defines the widget. The file bears the same name as the WIDGET file. Resources folder - Stores all the resource files that comprise the widget, which may include images, JS files, and a.PLIST file. However, WIDGET files may also store resource files outside of the folder with the KON file. You can open WIDGET files with Yahoo! Widgets in Windows and macOS. However, Yahoo! discontinued Yahoo! Widgets in 2012. Extracting the contents of a WIDGET file with a Zip utility WIDGET file open in Microsoft File Explorer If you would like to open a WIDGET file to extract its contents, you can use a Zip utility, such as Microsoft File Explorer (Windows), Apple Archive Utility (macOS), or Corel WinZip (Windows). You may need to rename the file extension from.widget to.zip for the Zip utility to recognize the file and decompress it. Windows Yahoo! Widgets —  (Discontinued). Microsoft File Explorer Included with OS. Corel WinZip. Mac Yahoo! Widgets —  (Discontinued). Apple Archive Utility Included with OS. Linux funzip. p7zipFree. Widget created in HybridJava, a language that combines HTML and Java into a single syntax; contains a function that can be called from a.PAGE file; may include multiple slots that serve as different steps within the function. HybridJava WIDGET files should be placed within the /​widgets subdirectory on the Web server. HybridJava Compiler has been discontinued. Windows HybridJava Compiler Text editor Mac HybridJava Compiler Text editor Linux HybridJava Compiler Text editor.",
    "Windows Microsoft Windows Mobile SDK. Microsoft Visual Studio with Windows Mobile Developer Tool Kit plug-in. Windows Yahoo! Widgets —  (Discontinued). Microsoft File Explorer Included with OS. Corel WinZip. Mac Yahoo! Widgets —  (Discontinued). Apple Archive Utility Included with OS. Linux funzip. p7zip. Windows HybridJava Compiler Text editor. Mac HybridJava Compiler Text editor. Linux HybridJava Compiler Text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["web_files",
    ".ITPC",
    "iTunes Podcast Link",
    "2.7",
    "An ITPC file is a link file used by Apple iTunes. It contains information that iTunes uses to subscribe a user to a podcast. Podcast creators host ITPC files online to help users easily subscribe to their podcasts. Most times, when a user wants to subscribe to a podcast, they either subscribe via that podcasts RSS feed or a feed link that uses the itpc:// protocol (instead of the http:// or https:// protocols). However, in some instances, a podcast creator may post an ITPC file online, which users can download, open, and use to subscribe to the creators podcast. If you have Apple iTunes (Windows) installed, you can double-click an ITPC file to open it in iTunes. After you double-click the file, iTunes should open and subscribe you to the podcast for which your ITPC file contains a link. Mac JRiver Media Center. Windows Apple iTunes —  (Discontinued). JRiver Media Center.",
    "Mac JRiver Media Center. Windows Apple iTunes —  (Discontinued). JRiver Media Center.",
    "?"],
    ["web_files",
    ".MAPX",
    "Mapjects Client Webparts File",
    "2.7",
    "File used by Mapjects GRYD, an operations visualization software package used for security management, energy management, and logistics; stores a client configuration of Webparts, which are widgets that can be mashed together to display integrated visualizations; used for configuring rich client Web interfaces. MAPX files reside on a Mapjects Web server and are parsed by the Web server to deliver the rich client content through a Web browser. They reference backend Mapjects.MAP data files, which contain the data and program code for the Webparts that are displayed. The Mapjects Web server supports rendering of HTML5, Silverlight, Flash, and PHP, as well as code for the ASP.NET Razor engine. NOTE: The Mapjects GRYD yPlug plug-in allows Mapjects GRYD to load third-party components. Windows Mapjects GRYD Mapjects GRYD with Mapjects yPlug plug-in Linux Mapjects GRYD Mapjects GRYD with Mapjects yPlug plug-in.",
    "Windows Mapjects GRYD Mapjects GRYD with Mapjects yPlug plug-in. Linux Mapjects GRYD Mapjects GRYD with Mapjects yPlug plug-in.",
    "?"],
